Reviewed `src/blog/people/drafts/Nara-Smith.md` and appended a `FRESH EYES REVIEW` block at the bottom. No body edits.

**The two biggest flags:**

1. **The Mormon conversion is completely absent.** She converted to the LDS church and married Lucky Blue Smith at 18, and that's a large part of *why* the tradwife label stuck — Ballerina Farm is the comparison critics actually make, and the draft never names it. The section titled "Why Nara Smith rejects the tradwife label" argues the label without touching the reason it landed. It's also a missed evidence win: her own line, *"I'm not in any way a hardcore Mormon… I don't wear garments. I didn't get married in the temple,"* is the same refusal-to-be-placed engine as the dishes line, and it's sitting unused.

2. **She's a Black woman and the draft never says so.** Born in Bloemfontein to a Mosotho mother and a German father. The childhood section gives the father a light-up globe and real texture; the mother gets a bad marriage and, later, chemo. And the racial angle is the sharpest contradiction in her story — a Black woman made poster child for an aesthetic whose "simpler time" legally excluded Black women from homemaking. That would do more for the tradwife section than any type argument currently in it.

Also flagged: the frame-ownership thesis is restated six times (locations listed), the Type 3 case runs twice at near-full length, the `Homemade` cookbook dropping two months after "I don't prioritize being a homemaker" is an unexploited contradiction, and "Nine years later" in the childhood section has no antecedent.

Sources checked: [Rolling Stone](https://www.rollingstone.com/culture/culture-features/nara-smith-lucky-blue-smith-tiktok-mormon-tradwife-1234979241/), [Axios SLC](https://www.axios.com/local/salt-lake-city/2024/03/07/nara-smith-mormon-tiktok-tradwife-model-cooking), [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nara_Smith), [TheGrio](https://thegrio.com/2026/08/03/why-nara-smith-and-the-tradwife-debate-has-struck-such-a-chord/)
